Now let me also double check my claimed B-9 facts once more:
1. Prop 4.1 Step 2: "$\|\Psi_S\|_{C^\alpha} = \max_{m\in S}\|\psi_m\|_{C^\alpha}$" — for $C^\alpha$ Hölder-Zygmund with disjoint supports separated $\ge 4\delta - \delta/2 > 0$: cross-support Hölder quotients of the TOP derivative: $|\nabla^a\psi_m(x) - \nabla^a\psi_{m'}(y)|/|x-y|^t \le 2\|\nabla^a\psi\|_\infty\delta^{-a}/(2\delta)^t = 2^{1-t}\delta^{-\alpha}\|\nabla^a\psi\|_\infty$; the single-bump seminorm is up to $\delta^{-\alpha}$; so cross terms are SAME order → equality fails but $\asymp$ holds with a constant $\le 2^{1-t}+1$. So $\|\Psi_S\|_{C^\alpha} \le C_0\delta^{-\alpha}$ with $C_0$ $(\alpha,d,\psi)$-dependent; κ picks up slack. Claimed strict equality is false in general; a specialist flags it. Confirmed minor-persistent.
2. Step 1: "$\|\psi_m\|_{C^\alpha} = \delta^{-\alpha}\|\psi\|_{C^\alpha}$" — dilation scales each derivative order separately; the top seminorm scales as $\delta^{-\alpha}$ but lower-order pieces scale as $\delta^{-j}$; equality to $\delta^{-\alpha}\|\psi\|_{C^\alpha}$ is false; $\asymp$ (for $\delta\le1$, top piece dominates) holds. Confirmed.
